#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "5b83575b-9674-5dc5-b3eb-a918f145be93" :question/math-academy-id "e-497" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2{x}}{x} \\textrm{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I \\\\ 3I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "72d0b4b3-bfc6-5339-809f-ff7cd63e4d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-498" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-I \\\\ 2I & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "f415cae9-6b18-53c1-8c47-6e0a1f0de59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7313" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2} e^x\\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I = \\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin(2x)\\, \\textrm{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos(2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I\\mathbin.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))}{5}-\\frac{{e}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0))}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi /2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25b6f859-5cb3-5948-ab8c-3481ddcd8dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316811} {:answer/id #uuid "91400871-fa51-578d-869f-dc6c636eba7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316812} {:answer/id #uuid "478633b1-dd47-5906-b835-61b996988d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316813} {:answer/id #uuid "fc92c06c-8884-5f85-b0e0-33c85be66bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316814} {:answer/id #uuid "cbebda66-c60f-5158-b671-df696ba491d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25b6f859-5cb3-5948-ab8c-3481ddcd8dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316811} :answer-field/id #uuid "110557a6-3102-584f-ae3a-b15976ab52e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "940ebb92-6c73-5c7e-8915-9f30454a9c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-114952" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2I \\\\ 3I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{12}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201e0256-9bec-58c2-b8b1-3043c4bad37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316818} {:answer/id #uuid "8387b868-ef66-5ee9-84d0-02aeed92438b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316819} {:answer/id #uuid "bd454877-806f-5580-8e3f-2d7fcfb8bf4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316820} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b5a851-e8f3-558a-b51b-ec135694ec4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316821} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf1c968-092e-5e80-a2c8-976ec521dcff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf1c968-092e-5e80-a2c8-976ec521dcff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316822} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6681d8-9d34-581c-8cc9-d311b45430d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f02d3181-2936-58a5-a039-94eff0391126" :question/math-academy-id "q-114965" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{6}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{ln}^{5}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{6}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{6{ln}^{5}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6I \\\\ 7I & ={ln}^{7}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{7}⁡x}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}⁡x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}⁡(e)-{ln}^{7}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ce7db0-39c3-5ce8-8b58-c2b7add9cd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316825} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3224dc-2808-536b-b49a-49ac7dd24874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316826} {:answer/id #uuid "19059a79-9956-586b-918e-1018529a7bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316827} {:answer/id #uuid "1f70ba3f-d391-5ae8-be76-9db025504ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316828} {:answer/id #uuid "4f60f33c-c465-51e4-a10c-671d2066ab02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f70ba3f-d391-5ae8-be76-9db025504ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316828} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f97fdf9-0efc-5c35-87ec-c19ec5c7fc0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aca29c8-5475-59ea-8c66-9b60c30722ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-114999" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{9}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{9}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})(\\frac{9{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9I \\\\ 10I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{40}({ln}^{10}⁡x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}({ln}^{10}⁡(e)-{ln}^{10}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bdafcfc-429c-5397-9871-6128556fb45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2x}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-{e}^{-x}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316832} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ac31ca-0410-5fb5-b4b5-8854a1c1f2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316833} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb366fc-b818-5853-9694-2d87eba5f9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316834} {:answer/id #uuid "45a1bffd-81dd-56f8-883f-edeec8f59c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316835} {:answer/id #uuid "11656843-8c50-52ec-955b-59126c0105e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11656843-8c50-52ec-955b-59126c0105e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316836} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce75557-f330-5e9d-b276-ab12594e1e61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34489983-3228-5451-8433-56868753d4c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-12919" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{9}I & =\\frac{3{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cda7138-9604-5f7a-b22a-9a860832a0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}(3\\cos (3x) - \\sin (3x)))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065753} {:answer/id #uuid "be7de30e-b7ff-5ce3-bba5-5ba9874a66f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}(3\\sin (3x) - \\cos (3x)))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065754} {:answer/id #uuid "169d6c83-6b3a-57ea-8bc3-2476ae6215f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}\\cos (3x) - 3\\sin (3x))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065755} {:answer/id #uuid "45eeec82-53f4-5815-be3b-5b8eab689391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}3\\cos (3x) - e^{-x}4\\sin (3x))/(5) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065756} {:answer/id #uuid "7444d42a-42fb-53f5-a3bc-978b57e668f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}(\\sin (3x) + 3\\cos (3x)))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be7de30e-b7ff-5ce3-bba5-5ba9874a66f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}(3\\sin (3x) - \\cos (3x)))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065754} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e101d3-f204-5a57-a1ea-d89637f4ea5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0920268-6184-5022-affb-309ac870c06c" :question/math-academy-id "q-14878" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =\\frac{3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "490f37fe-3aca-59cf-b01d-ff2ddc1c387c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316839} {:answer/id #uuid "bad78a0e-8ca6-5121-93ea-dd0fcbfb432e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316840} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fe1e70-4ad6-5066-be68-cde45d36d26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316841} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf0e015-4593-5067-a937-4d1e248dfb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316842} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef47da1-b52c-5e48-9d99-d8ad05f189dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ef47da1-b52c-5e48-9d99-d8ad05f189dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316843} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f314f5-f770-5b71-bc5f-18b0a68b5b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4cd3af2f-abf3-5cb0-ad69-153d9969ceaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-15409" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4992c51-9308-526a-a554-01e4225bb6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316846} {:answer/id #uuid "407a1e16-433d-5cd8-a662-30556351fb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{7}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316847} {:answer/id #uuid "515d424d-f021-58a0-8d23-43b60f11395e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316848} {:answer/id #uuid "cd0c5f0f-dd21-594b-9278-cab6f9b005d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{37}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316849} {:answer/id #uuid "3d58f10f-2331-56a2-80af-737e14af4ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd0c5f0f-dd21-594b-9278-cab6f9b005d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{37}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316849} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d5ab2f-2bd0-5928-8590-ee841f54ef4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83d98efe-1ed1-5349-93e2-811fc076fc47" :question/math-academy-id "q-15421" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{6x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{e}^{6x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(6{e}^{6x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{6x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{e}^{6x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(6{e}^{6x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6({e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-36I \\\\ 37I & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{37}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7be36c3-2e63-5e50-a917-21fa8a0fb77b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7be36c3-2e63-5e50-a917-21fa8a0fb77b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316853} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bd436f7-c07c-5fa8-94c9-33dcb2ea1853" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d787e1b-6005-525c-b60a-10567e5eaf1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-348618" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{5}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5{ln}^{4}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{5}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})(\\frac{5{ln}^{4}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{4}-5\\int \\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{4}-5I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{4}-5I \\\\ 6I & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{6}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{6}⁡({e}^{3})-{ln}^{6}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}((3\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{6}-{0}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({3}^{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9c3b835-2fb6-5a86-9974-38ca46832f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316856} {:answer/id #uuid "bf80b704-2d1d-5182-ac36-f6dacc958325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf80b704-2d1d-5182-ac36-f6dacc958325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316857} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a30074-00e7-5cfa-bf12-ea4dac172a56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96b50352-18f5-5bbb-a700-23a71998ea44" :question/math-academy-id "q-348849" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{8x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{8})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8}-2I \\\\ 3I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{8} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{3}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({ln}^{3}⁡({e}^{3})-{ln}^{3}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}((3\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({3}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d89fc349-65b1-5765-ba47-5be3d2fd3e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3\\pi/2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316860} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e8fccf-c196-54dc-88a9-2881afc199ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3\\pi/2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e8fccf-c196-54dc-88a9-2881afc199ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3\\pi/2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316861} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9180b05-50c3-55d8-84d4-6df068d68406" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f59147ac-89e8-5b49-99ae-b5e4107f553c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348955" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{3}{13}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(3{e}^{3x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{3}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(3{e}^{3x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{3}{2}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{3}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{2}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{9}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{4}I & ={e}^{3x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3(\\pi /2)}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )))-(\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3(0)}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}{e}^{3\\pi /2}(2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1))-\\frac{3}{13} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{13}({e}^{3\\pi /2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2db65a-fc37-5636-a429-5d5db60c66e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e^{-4\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e2db65a-fc37-5636-a429-5d5db60c66e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e^{-4\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316864} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e9027a4-4ea8-508a-bfd3-4a0e2eddd4df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b48f842-2d47-5937-8d3e-e594261f9f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-349030" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})\\,(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-4x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{10}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{10}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{10}(0+1))-(-\\frac{1}{10}(0+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(1-{e}^{-4\\pi }).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832df126-b4de-51d1-9f54-625406c82c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-\\pi}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "832df126-b4de-51d1-9f54-625406c82c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-\\pi}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316867} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e3ff65-9784-5471-a33f-a1ee5383e36f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae8972c6-41c0-5cb2-9053-0f9dfe222d7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-362669" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =({e}^{-x})(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{10}(0-(-1)))-(\\frac{1}{10}(0-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{-\\pi }+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210e166e-60ee-5c66-93fc-2a2a1f067bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316870} {:answer/id #uuid "f106a948-5ba6-5ac5-bdd9-7b320ff4d9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316871} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f4d064-7e6c-5231-b016-de9ade2872fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316872} {:answer/id #uuid "077d3f97-da7c-50e7-abe2-d81ac948568f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316873} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9b0efe-3d56-5862-a13d-99b4e8b336a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca9b0efe-3d56-5862-a13d-99b4e8b336a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316874} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e01866d-e3fa-546d-a8a6-b2409a025a36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f752a5a-deed-56d6-992b-90e858e58c9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49609" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a55290bf-4acd-50db-a97a-451fe5e72f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316877} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8ee124-605e-53e8-b56e-b6859bd07dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316878} {:answer/id #uuid "230c0e1c-c427-5c95-ac60-fb67a6b56cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316879} {:answer/id #uuid "93ba6ba6-77f3-5e54-86c5-1426d51448d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316880} {:answer/id #uuid "97af8afe-75c6-53bc-ab56-3a9c9e418456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10({e}^{6\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55290bf-4acd-50db-a97a-451fe5e72f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316877} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b9391da-4dbb-5d79-b473-25c72a571962" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f00816b-dc7a-5ff5-aa98-8c002fd0740e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49967" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3J \\\\ I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3(-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{10}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2\\pi )}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))}{10}-\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{6\\pi }}{10}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db2f0f4b-6198-5752-9c20-e9f98205b990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316884} {:answer/id #uuid "861c386c-8a73-5b67-9f6a-02555a852b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(2{e}^{2\\pi }+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316885} {:answer/id #uuid "27f5778c-e151-5b58-9e42-a27103dbf21d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316886} {:answer/id #uuid "8f211399-e171-56f0-93e5-d10f3158739a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2\\pi }+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316887} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1a9da2-1578-59f7-8914-cb2658c2bf39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27f5778c-e151-5b58-9e42-a27103dbf21d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316886} :answer-field/id #uuid "02002b45-744b-5be3-bc93-56a680ffef79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db2f5081-4388-5d02-89b1-d05db7e0dc4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83012" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
